TY  - JOUR
TI  - From Single Archetype to Multi-Sample Approach for Stock-Level Retrofit Predictions
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Lee, S. J.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - It remains questionable whether a single archetype possesses sufficient representativeness of actual building stock and fully captures the diversity of the building population. This is particularly relevant when quantifying the impact of energy conservation measures (ECMs) for large-scale energy retrofits by policymakers. This study examined this limitation using detailed EnergyPlus models of 362 real-world healthcare buildings and proposed a multi-sample approach for seven ECM scenarios. It was found that any archetype building is not likely to reproduce the effects of stock-level retrofit across all ECMs. The potential of the multi-sample approach was also evaluated using sampled building subsets of different sizes, and substantial improvement was observed with as few as three buildings. These findings suggest that rather than the precise definition of a single archetype, the key issue for retrofit-oriented building energy modeling is the use of a small set of sampled buildings that preserve within-stock variation.
KW  - archetype
KW  - multi-sample
KW  - retrofit
KW  - energy conservation measure
KW  - wisdom of crowds
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Measured vs. Simulated post-retrofit energy-use in 149 public buildings using dynamic simulation
AU  - Lee, S. J.
AU  - Jo, H. G.
AU  - Yoo, Y. S.
AU  - Park, C. S.
PY  - under review
AB  - Dynamic building energy simulation is widely used to examine “what-if” questions about how changes in building envelopes and systems affect energy use, particularly in retrofit evaluation. Yet measured energy-use changes in real buildings are shaped by both physical changes and uncertainties that are not fully represented in simulation models, making it unclear how simulation-based “what-if” results should be understood when measured–simulated discrepancies exist. Despite this limitation, the usefulness of simulation has predominantly been discussed in terms of its agreement with measured energy use at the individual-building level, while its explanatory value for what-if changes under real-world conditions has received less attention. In this study, this issue was evaluated by comparing measured data and EnergyPlus results for 149 retrofitted public non-residential buildings in South Korea, including daycare and healthcare buildings. Before- and after-retrofit EnergyPlus models were developed by integrating drawings, specifications, surveys, site-visit records, the Korean building energy code, and actual weather data. The simulation results were then compared with measured data in terms of energy use and before–after changes at the individual-building level, by energy conservation measure (ECM)-combination groups, and at the building-stock level. Results show that discrepancies between measured and simulated energy use were substantial at the individual-building level, whereas measured and simulated post-retrofit energy-use changes were similar at the building-stock level, both annually and monthly. This contrast was particularly evident for healthcare buildings, where the measured–simulated discrepancies in absolute annual energy use were 1,559.3 MWh/yr before retrofit and 1,346.0 MWh/yr after retrofit, whereas the discrepancy in annual energy savings was substantially smaller, at 213.3 MWh/yr. These findings suggest that before–after differencing and stock-level aggregation can help reduce the influence of building-specific deviations, allowing dynamic simulation to serve as a useful interpretive framework for understanding retrofit-induced energy-use changes in real buildings. Such an interpretation can extend the role of dynamic simulation in stock-level retrofit evaluation and decision-making under real-world uncertainty, particularly for policymakers and retrofit decision-makers, even when discrepancies remain for individual buildings.
KW  - bbuilding energy simulation
KW  - dynamic simulation
KW  - energy retrofit
KW  - energy use
KW  - performance gap
